#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204696} :activity/id #uuid "5be4f1e2-25eb-55ea-aadd-67a12587848d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048805 :topic/id #uuid "c10b84fd-2e2a-576b-a2ce-4b2fb0bbd333" :topic/math-academy-id 4820} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205233 :tutorial/content "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is called **reflexive** if $(x,x) \\in R$ for every element $x \\in A.$ In other words, each element of $A$ is related to itself by $R.$\n\nFor example:\n\n- Any integer $x$ is congruent to itself modulo $n$ for any natural $n.$\n\n$$\nx\\equiv x \\quad(\\textrm{mod } n)\n$$\n\nSo, congruence of integers is reflexive.\n\n- The relation \"are born in the same year,\" defined on the set containing the entire human population, is also reflexive. This is because any person was born in the same year as themselves.\n\nA binary relation $R$ on a set $A$ is called **irreflexive** if $(x,x) \\notin R$ for every element $x \\in A.$ In other words, no element of $A$ is related to itself by $R.$\n\nFor example:\n\n- The relation \"is less than\" $(\\lt),$ defined on the set of real numbers, is irreflexive since no number is less than itself. In other words, the statement\n\n$$\nx < x\n$$\n\nis *false* for any $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\n- The relation \"is the sisters of,\" defined on the set containing all people, is irreflexive. This is because a person can't be their own sister.\n\nA relation *cannot* be both reflexive and irreflexive simultaneously. However, most relations are neither reflexive nor irreflexive. \n\nFor example, consider relation $x \\: R \\: y,$ defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$ as $xy \\lt 10{:}$ \n\n- $R$ is not reflexive since $5 \\cdot 5 \\not\\lt 10,$ which means that $5$ is not related to itself.\n\n- $R$ is not irreflexive since $1 \\cdot 1 \\lt 10,$ which means that $1$ is related to itself.\n\nWe can summarize all the above in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f6/f60867376793fc07fce1dd398e00a0c6fd3051cce95ee98f1fe48b843dab4539.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a55d5d6-f98a-5513-81ed-d4efe8b65c1f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "da34e1bc-8af1-5b2d-8dac-8eaec4ac9595" :step/math-academy-id 156626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205245 :tutorial/content "Let's describe the properties of adjacency matrices for reflexive and irreflexive relations.\n\n- A finite binary relation is reflexive if and only if its adjacency matrix has only $1$'s on the main diagonal (from top-left to bottom-right). \n\nFor example, the relation $R = \\big\\{ (1,1), (1,2), (2,2), (3,3) \\big\\},$ defined on $\\{1,2,3\\},$ is reflexive, and its adjacency matrix is the following:\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n\n- A finite binary relation is irreflexive if and only if its adjacency matrix has only $0$'s on the main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the relation $R = \\big\\{ (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) \\big\\},$ defined on $\\{1,2,3\\},$ is irreflexive, and its adjacency matrix is the following:\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\nFor relations that are neither reflexive nor irreflexive, the corresponding adjacency matrix will have both $0$'s and $1$'s on the main diagonal." :tutorial/id #uuid "83746cea-3ae4-593b-a187-818e0a70f1fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9106 :tutorial/title "Adjacency Matrices of Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations"} :step/id #uuid "29ac53a0-4b57-542f-bc84-1eb3e33e2bde" :step/math-academy-id 156627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203921 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203687 :question/id #uuid "058f91e1-7365-5ee5-8c35-0eaa433db282" :question/math-academy-id "e-14616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80cffe94-4f90-5bd8-b0b3-c09d54100580" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048960}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416862}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations"} :step/id #uuid "5f7a00fd-5866-5b89-8f7f-4439a3468c7a" :step/math-academy-id 156628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205244 :tutorial/content "Plots of reflexive and irreflexive relations have the following properties:\n\n- A binary relation is reflexive if and only if its plot contains all the corresponding points on the line $y=x.$ \n\nFor example, the relation $R = \\big\\{ (1,1), (1,2), (2,2), (3,3) \\big\\},$ defined on $\\{1,2,3\\},$ is reflexive, and its plot contains all the diagonal points, as shown below:\n\n![](images/2c/2c7f9c6eb18ca50f58a9f214c31ab3d80e6fd652f048f3502aafc06e7e330687.png)\n\n \n\n- A binary relation is irreflexive if and only if its plot does *not* contain *any* points on the line $y=x.$ \n\nFor example, the relation $R = \\big\\{ (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) \\big\\},$ defined on $\\{1,2,3\\},$ is irreflexive, and its plot does not contain *any* diagonal points, as shown below:\n\n![](images/33/33d033652c69df776b911c742c6f8f81b0c4bba81c50bf5aba2118877ed75c73.png)\n\n \n\nFor relations that are neither reflexive nor irreflexive, the corresponding plot will have some points on the line $y=x$ but not all of them." :tutorial/id #uuid "4ae07891-12e0-5695-9802-4984c88d0ebb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9105 :tutorial/title "Plots of Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations"} :step/id #uuid "0caa4833-c517-545c-8a09-7e81456e0909" :step/math-academy-id 156631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205246 :tutorial/content "Digraphs of reflexive and irreflexive relations have the following properties:\n\n- A binary relation is reflexive if and only if its digraph has a loop at every node. \n\nFor example, the relation $R = \\big\\{ (1,1), (1,2), (2,2), (3,3) \\big\\},$ defined on $\\{1,2,3\\},$ is reflexive, and its digraph has a loop at each node, as shown below:\n\n![](images/8b/8b4b6d97ba6901b72fbde16bc992887ed9db05cd3b522c1899a177070a2afe38.png)\n\n \n\n- A binary relation is irreflexive if and only if its digraph does *not* contain *any* loops at the nodes. \n\nFor example, the relation $R = \\big\\{ (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) \\big\\},$ defined on $\\{1,2,3\\},$ is irreflexive, and its digraph has no loops, as shown below:\n\n![](images/43/432e41e141ecdcd95b6f1bbe6e94a411751806b48675c2eca98a9d625e94d4f1.png)\n\n \n\nFor relations that are neither reflexive nor irreflexive, the corresponding digraph will have loops at some nodes but not at all of them." :tutorial/id #uuid "9754c540-4570-5492-bd2e-9e410bd36457" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9107 :tutorial/title "Digraphs of Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations"} :step/id #uuid "4e2d5c41-9b2c-5e35-b90a-3bfff435f192" :step/math-academy-id 156632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204701}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203688 :question/id #uuid "5a1a0f7a-d39c-5551-a512-5fd1bea5b3cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-14620"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b47a31-7bda-5366-bac4-1d070776e776" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048710}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420406}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "14c54821-f810-5b6c-accf-888233c06a21" :step/math-academy-id 156633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204702}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203689 :question/id #uuid "76419f6e-45a2-5af2-b048-2f7c323db796" :question/math-academy-id "e-14621"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "306be17f-f950-57e2-9cb7-2b33c8a4929e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050176}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416861}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations Over Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "a2483a46-faf2-51f8-9a43-787f833d454c" :step/math-academy-id 156636}] :activity/title "Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204112}]}
